ACTION: REVISE
TITLE: Evaluating Representation Reusability in Unsupervised Fine-Tuning of Vision Foundation Models
REVISED PROPOSAL: Instead of using Adam optimization for baseline 2 in "Investigation of Input Space Smoothing for Unsupervised Visual Adaptation", use a continuation learning approach to fine-tune the pre-trained model on the target dataset with input space smoothing. This will assess whether the pre-trained representations can be effectively reused as a starting point for unsupervised fine-tuning.
CONTROLS: 
 Baseline 1: No smoothing;
 Baseline 2: Standard Adam optimization for pre-training; 
 Baseline 3: Supervised fine-tuning; 
 Baseline 4: Continuation learning with input space smoothing.
COST: 12
PREDICTION: Continuation learning approach with input space smoothing should improve the performance of the pre-trained model on the target task and demonstrate reusability of the pre-trained representations. This approach might require careful tuning of hyperparameters and the rate of continuation learning.

ACTION: ADD
TITLE: Transfer Learning with Adapters for Unsupervised Visual Adaptation
PROPOSAL: Investigate the use of adapters for transfer learning to adapt pre-trained visual foundation models to new data distributions without supervision. Use a combination of synthetic and real-world object-centric videos for evaluation, and measure the performance of the adapted model on object recognition tasks. Explore the effect of different adapter architectures, adapter initialization methods, and adaptation techniques on the performance.
CONTROLS: 
 Baseline 1: Standard pre-trained model without adapters;
 Baseline 2: Standard pre-trained model with adapters and random initialization;
 Baseline 3: Supervised fine-tuning with adapters;
 Baseline 4: Unsupervised fine-tuning with adapters and different initialization methods.
COST: 75
PREDICTION: Using adapters to transfer learn the pre-trained model to the new data distribution could be an effective way to adapt without supervision, and its performance would be comparable to standard pre-trained model fine-tuning on large amounts of labeled data. However, it might require careful tuning of adapter architectures and adaptation techniques to avoid over-adaptation.

ACTION: ADD
TITLE: Multi-modal Self-Supervised Learning for Unsupervised Visual Adaptation
PROPOSAL: Investigate the use of multi-modal self-supervised learning for adapting pre-trained visual foundation models to new data distributions without supervision. Use a combination of vision and other modalities (e.g., audio, text) extracted from object-centric videos to improve adaptation and explore the effect of different multi-modal settings on adaptation performance.
CONTROLS: 
 Baseline 1: Standard vision-only pre-trained model;
 Baseline 2: Multi-modal pre-trained model without additional self-supervised learning;
 Baseline 3: Supervised fine-tuning with multi-modal settings;
 Baseline 4: Unsupervised fine-tuning with multi-modal settings and different weight initialization methods.
COST: 120
PREDICTION: Using multi-modal self-supervised learning for adaptation could improve the ability of the pre-trained model to generalize across various scenarios and strengthen its capability to handle uncertainty and ambiguity, especially when the target domain contains multimodal sensory information. However, it might require careful tuning of the relative importance of the different modalities to avoid numerical instability or dominance from one modality over others.

ACTION: ADD
TITLE: Robustness Analysis of Unsupervised Visual Adaptation Methods Through Adversarial Training
PROPOSAL: Investigate the robustness of different unsupervised visual adaptation methods by incorporating adversarial training to improve their resilience to potential distribution shifts in object-centric videos. Measure the performance and attack transferability of the adapted models in scenarios with both clean and adversarially perturbed input data.
CONTROLS: 
 Baseline 1: Standard pre-trained model;
 Baseline 2: Pre-trained model fine-tuned without adversarial training;
 Baseline 3: Adversarial-trained model without adaptation;
 Baseline 4: Adaptation with standard training and different levels of adversarial training.
COST: 150
PREDICTION: The adapted models with adversarial training would be more robust and less susceptible to distribution shifts caused by data perturbations, thus likely providing state-of-the-art performance on task-driven metrics, even under uncertainty. However, training a robust model could come at the cost of increased model complexity and over-adaptation.

ACTION: ADD
TITLE: Automatic Curriculum Learning for Unsupervised Visual Adaptation
PROPOSAL: Investigate the application of automatic curriculum learning to unsupervised visual adaptation methods, where the training process is progressively guided by a sequence of expert-crafted tasks that aim to adapt the pre-trained model to the target data distribution. Measure the performance and stability of the adapted model across different curriculum settings.
CONTROLS: 
 Baseline 1: Standard pre-trained model;
 Baseline 2: Standard fine-tuning without automatic curriculum learning;
 Baseline 3: Curricula constructed through reinforcement learning;
 Baseline 4: Adaptive curriculum learning that adjusts in real-time to the model's performance.
COST: 180
PREDICTION: The automatically learned curriculum could adaptively adjust the learning process of the model to be more suitable for the target task, leading to better performance on the final task, especially for more complex and diverse target datasets. However, the optimal curriculum for the final task might be difficult to find via a globally optimal search, potentially resulting in suboptimal results or requiring significant computational resources to find the optimal solution.
